{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demonstrations without a validated common rule

This instance supplied three sharply different pairs: a 21×21 sparse diagonal-color scene became a full 5-color periodic field; a 23×23 otherwise blank grid with two bottom stripes became an alternating two-color row pattern; and a 2×2 near-uniform patch had its lone zero filled. The 12×12 test instead contained offset diagonal tracks in colors 8, 2, 6, and 7. The demonstrations were evidence of radically different surface transformations, but no cumulative exact-delta analysis produced a validated shared selector or output mapping. Serial demo requests (three were used) did not resolve the relationship; the last action continued requesting rather than making an evidence-based decision.

Reusable lesson: task IDs and stream membership alone do not justify assuming a common pixel-level operation. Record each pair's exact delta and output structure before requesting another. Explicitly test whether the task may be an abstract transformation shared across different visual classes; formulate at least two candidate abstractions and derive predictions that can be replayed against all examples. If no candidate maps all demonstrations exactly, another request is justified only if its predicted outcomes discriminate those candidates. Do not invent a test output by blending the demo patterns or select one demo's operation based on superficial resemblance. Keep the action protocol strict: one JSON action only on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md", "content": "# Compact-output reassessment after incorrect submissions

When inputs are partitioned by repeated separator rows or columns but demos produce much smaller outputs, dimension ratios help identify possible structure but do not establish the encoding. Keep distinct hypotheses for (a) summarizing interior regions, (b) recording separator/boundary markers at their intersections, and (c) selecting only rows or columns with special markers. For each demo, write down the exact partition counts and map candidate input coordinates or blocks to each output cell, including ordering. Reproduce every output exactly before transferring the map.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, do not merely alter colors or copy another visually salient set of markers. Reopen the task class and output representation, and re-derive dimensions and correspondence from all demos. Repeated, heterogeneous demos may share only an abstract structure; spend another demo only when two explicit mappings predict a materially different discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-20746363-review.md", "content": "# Session review: compact-output task with varied demonstrations

This session had a test grid partitioned by separator rows/columns, while the demonstrations varied widely in size, palette, and output dimensions. The solver did not establish a consistent tile-to-output correspondence or exact encoding from any demo. It guessed constant/compact patterns, submitted a repeated 4x2 candidate after it was rejected, and requested several more demonstrations without adequately integrating their evidence. Those actions produced incorrect answers and unnecessary cost.

Reusable lesson: for compact partitioned outputs, explicitly enumerate panels, separators, output shape, marker coordinates/counts, and output values for every demo. Keep dimensions, correspondence, selector, and encoding as independent hypotheses. A changed palette/scale across demos may still admit an abstract shared mapping, but do not infer one from appearance. Replay any proposed rule exactly across every complete demo before submitting. After a wrong submission, discard the entire failed rule; never repeat the same grid. Request a demo only when two explicit live hypotheses predict different, answer-relevant results. Maintain the Continual-ARC contract: exactly one legal JSON action on the final line on every turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-20746363-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-f0caf098-intersection-ledger.md", "content": "# Divider-crossing demos with varying compact outputs

This task family presented large regular lattices whose outputs were much smaller, with marked values placed on divider intersections. Across five examples, input/output dimensions varied substantially (23×29→5×4, 26×29→3×4, 22×30→1×1, 28×23→3×3, 29×14→5×4). The outputs echoed some values found at marked crossings, but the examples did not establish a direct full crossing-matrix extraction or a unique rule for edge inclusion, ordering, or default values. The 24×28 test's lattice appearance therefore did not justify a specific compact output.

Reusable response: maintain an explicit coordinate ledger of divider lines, exceptional intersections, and every output cell; compare candidate direct, cropped/selected, and panel-summary mappings against all demos. Before another request, state two competing mappings and a decision-relevant predicted observation. If no concrete discriminator exists, do not chain requests or submit a guess based only on visual resemblance. This is unresolved evidence, not a solved transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-f0caf098-intersection-ledger.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/stream-expansion-and-failed-stamp.md", "content": "# Stream expansion demos and representation checks

In a task with a 5×10 test, the first two demos had 1D inputs and expanded outputs: 9×1 → 27×2, with each input value repeated across each output row and the sequence arranged as reversed/original/reversed; 1×15 → 3×30, with three identical rows and a sequence arranged as reversed/original. These establish that 1D-to-2D expansion depends on orientation, but do not establish a mapping for a genuinely 2D test.

A speculative answer incorrectly treated the 5×10 test as a 3×2 stamp replication over groups of three rows. The key failure was unsupported transfer of a construction despite the test not matching either demonstrated input class. After the incorrect response, the correct next step is to reopen output representation and seek evidence that distinguishes candidate 2D mappings, rather than cosmetically modifying the stamp. For demos, articulate what the new pair would disambiguate before paying the request cost; requests should resolve a concrete uncertainty.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/stream-expansion-and-failed-stamp.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/continual-turn-lessons.md", "content": "# Continual ARC turn completeness and evidence boundaries

In continual puzzle interfaces, each message—including incorrect feedback—requires one legal action JSON on the final line. A thoughtful diagnosis without a valid action leaves the turn incomplete.

After a rejection, use references from previously encountered sessions only as process cautions. They are not evidence that this task shares the referenced transformation; do not transplant specific rules or guessed output grids. Reconstruct the current examples' deltas and representations, and demand exact replay of every available demo before another submission. If no supported candidate exists, request a demo only when it answers a concrete ambiguity that could change the output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/continual-turn-lessons.md"}
